{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>The token map, and where it quietly breaks</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Beyond passwords: <code>autocomplete="email"</code>, <code>autocomplete="tel"</code>, <code>autocomplete="name"</code>, <code>autocomplete="street-address"</code>, and the payment triad <code>autocomplete="cc-number"</code>, <code>autocomplete="cc-exp"</code>, <code>autocomplete="cc-csc"</code> all follow the same pattern — a specific token per field purpose, not a generic on/off switch. Every field that autofill should ever touch benefits from getting its specific token, not just password fields.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A common regression: a signup form correctly uses <code>new-password</code> on the password field, then a "confirm password" field right next to it is left with no <code>autocomplete</code> at all (or copy-pasted as <code>current-password</code> by mistake) — some managers then fill the confirm field with an old saved password instead of the newly generated one, producing a mismatch the user has to debug themselves. Both password fields in a signup form need <code>new-password</code>, not just the first one.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      <code>autocomplete="off"</code> on a password field is explicitly not a reliable way to prevent saving in modern browsers — most major browsers ignore <code>off</code> for login and password fields specifically, on the reasoning that letting sites opt out of password-manager support outright is worse for user security than respecting the site's preference. If a field genuinely shouldn't be saved (a one-time admin-impersonation password, say), the more effective mitigation is a fresh, randomly-suffixed <code>name</code> attribute the manager has never seen before, not the <code>autocomplete</code> attribute — that's a meaningfully different mechanism from choosing the correct token for a field that should autofill.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

