{# ── Chapter: Takeaway ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="takeaway">
  <h2>Remember</h2>
  <div class="case-takeaway">
    <h3>Key takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <div class="tone-junior">A password field with no autocomplete value, or a generic one, doesn't get autofilled or offered a generated password — use autocomplete="current-password" for login and autocomplete="new-password" for signup/change-password fields.</div>
        <div class="tone-mid">Give every autofill-relevant field its specific token (email, current-password, new-password, one-time-code, and the address/payment tokens) — labels and placeholders are for humans, autocomplete is what autofill actually reads.</div>
        <div class="tone-staff">autocomplete="off" doesn't reliably block saving on password fields in modern browsers; use both new-password fields consistently in a signup form, and don't rely on this attribute for anything that needs to be truly excluded from autofill.</div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
